5. Exponential Integral and Related Functions
Continuous (x>0):
5.1. Exponential Integral
Definitions
5.1.1   E_{1}(z)=\int_{-x}^{\infty}\frac{e^{-t}}{t}dt\qquad(|\arg z|<\pi)
5.1.2   Ei(x)=-\int_{-x}^{\infty}\frac{e^{-t}}{t}dt=\int_{-\infty}^{x}\frac{e^{t}}{t}dt\qquad(x>0)
5.1.3   li(x)=\frac{\Gamma e^{\alpha}}{\beta_{n}}\frac{dt}{n}=\mathrm{Ei}(\ln x)\qquad(x>1)
5.1.4   E_{\alpha}(z)=\int_{1}^{\infty}\frac{e^{-zt}}{t^{\eta}}dt\qquad(n=0,1,2,\dots;\Re z>0)
5.1.5   a_{\alpha}(z)=\int_{1}^{\infty}t^{\eta}e^{-rt}dt\qquad(n=0,1,2,\dots;\Re z>0)
5.1.6   b_{\alpha}(z)=\int_{-1}^{1}t^{\eta}e^{-rt}dt\qquad(n=0,1,2,\dots)
In 5.1.1 it is assumed that the path of integration excludes the origin and does not cross the negative real axis.

Analytic continuation of the functions in 5.1.1, 5.1.2, and 5.1.4 for n>0 yields multi-valued functions with branch points at z=0 and z=0.3 They are single-valued functions in the z-plane cut along the negative real axis.4 The function li(z), the logarithmic integral, has an additional branch point at z=1.

Footnote 4: Some authors [5.14], [5.16] use the entire function \int_{0}^{\infty}(1-e^{-\eta})dt/t as the basic function and denote it by Ein(z). We have Ein(x)=E_{1}(x)+\ln x+\gamma.

Footnote 4: Various authors define the integral \int_{-m}^{z}\left(e^{t/\eta}dt\right) in the z-plane cut along the positive real axis and denote it also by Eli(x). For z=x>0 additional notations such as El(x) (e.g., in [5.10], [5.25]), E*(x) (in [5.2], Eli*(x) in [5.6]) are then used to designate the principal value of the integral. Correspondingly, E_{1}(x) is often denoted by −Ei(-x).

